window.__programmeIndexChunk("statements",{"e0dd3eb93ea130c4c738ce9ba14df0c9fa87613b58ea65cb1921ad87c9a9397e":{"markdown":"**Theorem 4.1 — Chern numbers.** Fix \\(n\\geq1\\). Suppose for each \\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\) a closed complex \\(j\\)-manifold \\(K_j\\) is chosen with \\(s_j(c)[K_j]\\ne0\\). For a partition \\(J=(j_1,\\ldots,j_q)\\) of \\(n\\), put \\(K_J=K_{j_1}\\times\\cdots\\times K_{j_q}\\). The matrix\n\\[\n\\bigl(c_I[K_J]\\bigr)_{I,J\\vdash n}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nis nonsingular over \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Its determinant has absolute value\n\\[\n\\prod_{J\\vdash n}\\ \\prod_{j\\in J}|s_j(c)[K_j]|.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nIn particular all the Chern-number functionals of complex dimension \\(n\\) are linearly independent over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), as witnessed by products of complex projective spaces.","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 4.1 — Chern numbers.<\/strong> Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq1\\)\">\\(n\\geq1\\)<\/span>. Suppose for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\)\">\\(1\\leq j\\leq n\\)<\/span> a closed complex <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>-manifold <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_j\\)\">\\(K_j\\)<\/span> is chosen with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_j(c)[K_j]\\ne0\\)\">\\(s_j(c)[K_j]\\ne0\\)<\/span>. For a partition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J=(j_1,\\ldots,j_q)\\)\">\\(J=(j_1,\\ldots,j_q)\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_J=K_{j_1}\\times\\cdots\\times K_{j_q}\\)\">\\(K_J=K_{j_1}\\times\\cdots\\times K_{j_q}\\)<\/span>. The matrix\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\bigl(c_I[K_J]\\bigr)_{I,J\\vdash n}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\bigl(c_I[K_J]\\bigr)_{I,J\\vdash n}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nis nonsingular over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb Q\\)\">\\(\\mathbb Q\\)<\/span>. Its determinant has absolute value\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\prod_{J\\vdash n}\\ \\prod_{j\\in J}|s_j(c)[K_j]|.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\prod_{J\\vdash n}\\ \\prod_{j\\in J}|s_j(c)[K_j]|.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nIn particular all the Chern-number functionals of complex dimension <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> are linearly independent over <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb Q\\)\">\\(\\mathbb Q\\)<\/span>, as witnessed by products of complex projective spaces.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/DG-CHAR/characteristic-numbers-and-projective-product-independence.md","start_offset":12617,"end_offset":13244,"label":"Theorem 4.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0da6a7c8cbd70cf4fbc2de99c48d209201e2a3e02c7d992983176d04243c5e8":{"markdown":"**Corollary 4.2.** For an arbitrary von Neumann algebra \\(M\\), the following are equivalent:\n\n1. \\(M\\) is injective.\n2. In one, hence every, faithful normal representation, multiplication \\(M\\odot M'\\to B(H)\\) is bounded for the minimal tensor norm.\n3. The identity is a pointwise ultraweak limit of cpc matrix factorizations \\(M\\to M_n\\to M\\), with normal incoming maps.\n4. The identity on \\(M_*\\) is a pointwise norm limit of cpc factorizations \\(M_*\\to M_n^*\\to M_*\\), in the dual matrix orders and Banach dual norms.\n\nThe factorizations in (3) also converge pointwise sigma-strong*, and their incoming maps can be chosen unital.","html":"<p><strong>Corollary 4.2.<\/strong> For an arbitrary von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, the following are equivalent:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is injective.<\/li>\n<li>In one, hence every, faithful normal representation, multiplication <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\odot M&#x27;\\to B(H)\\)\">\\(M\\odot M&#x27;\\to B(H)\\)<\/span> is bounded for the minimal tensor norm.<\/li>\n<li>The identity is a pointwise ultraweak limit of cpc matrix factorizations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\to M_n\\to M\\)\">\\(M\\to M_n\\to M\\)<\/span>, with normal incoming maps.<\/li>\n<li>The identity on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> is a pointwise norm limit of cpc factorizations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\to M_n^*\\to M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\to M_n^*\\to M_*\\)<\/span>, in the dual matrix orders and Banach dual norms.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The factorizations in (3) also converge pointwise sigma-strong*, and their incoming maps can be chosen unital.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-APPROX/src/averaging-crossed-products-injectivity.md","start_offset":153714,"end_offset":154348,"label":"Corollary 4.2","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0c58a3a3dcb0e90e5948436c00212cb589b6254574926c81c784cadb439f37c":{"markdown":"**Corollary 5.3.** An infinite product of finite factors in their normalized trace GNS representations is a finite factor with its product trace. If infinitely many component factors are nontrivial, it is of type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\).","html":"<p><strong>Corollary 5.3.<\/strong> An infinite product of finite factors in their normalized trace GNS representations is a finite factor with its product trace. If infinitely many component factors are nontrivial, it is of type <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)\">\\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-APPROX/src/infinite-tensor-products.md","start_offset":33060,"end_offset":33293,"label":"Corollary 5.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e061c543effeb7f24d4ec194658d0a9b2e86cff9ca8ef03cc2c013ab99e27369":{"markdown":"**Corollary 1.2.** A bounded sequence lies in \\(N_\\infty\\) if and only if it lies in \\(N_\\omega\\) for every free ultrafilter \\(\\omega\\).","html":"<p><strong>Corollary 1.2.<\/strong> A bounded sequence lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_\\infty\\)\">\\(N_\\infty\\)<\/span> if and only if it lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_\\omega\\)\">\\(N_\\omega\\)<\/span> for every free ultrafilter <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-APPROX/src/ordinary-multipliers-and-predual-compactness.md","start_offset":4086,"end_offset":4224,"label":"Corollary 1.2","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e02c3fd1ae4d99f4a7ee20b8c45610f6d9220782cfbc012d187041ce9a8127eb":{"markdown":"**Lemma 5 (products and adjoints).** Suppose $X$ is a C*-algebra and $\\alpha$ consists of star automorphisms. For compact $K,L$,\n\n$$X_\\alpha(K)X_\\alpha(L)\\subseteq X_\\alpha(K+L),\\qquad\nX_\\alpha(K)^*=X_\\alpha(-K). \\tag{18}$$\n\nIn particular $S_r=X_\\alpha([r,\\infty))$ satisfies $S_rS_s\\subseteq S_{r+s}$.","html":"<p><strong>Lemma 5 (products and adjoints).<\/strong> Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is a C*-algebra and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\)<\/span> consists of star automorphisms. For compact <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K,L\\)\">\\(K,L\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[X_\\alpha(K)X_\\alpha(L)\\subseteq X_\\alpha(K+L),\\qquad\nX_\\alpha(K)^*=X_\\alpha(-K). \\tag{18}\\]\">\\[X_\\alpha(K)X_\\alpha(L)\\subseteq X_\\alpha(K+L),\\qquad\nX_\\alpha(K)^*=X_\\alpha(-K). \\tag{18}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_r=X_\\alpha([r,\\infty))\\)\">\\(S_r=X_\\alpha([r,\\infty))\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_rS_s\\subseteq S_{r+s}\\)\">\\(S_rS_s\\subseteq S_{r+s}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L35.md","start_offset":16774,"end_offset":17078,"label":"Lemma 5","extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"e0d35bbf80c73d9c06e5e5900f68a964f0436e644d0c8602466f701129ac89d7":{"markdown":"**Theorem 5.1.** For a bounded derivation $d$, $e^{td}$ is an algebra automorphism for every real $t$. It is a \\*-automorphism for every real $t$ if $d$ is a \\*-derivation. Conversely a uniformly continuous group of \\*-automorphisms of $A$ is $\\alpha_t=e^{td}$ for a unique bounded \\*-derivation $d$, and\n\n$$d=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\alpha_h-I}{h}\\quad\\hbox{in operator norm}.$$","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 5.1.<\/strong> For a bounded derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{td}\\)\">\\(e^{td}\\)<\/span> is an algebra automorphism for every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>. It is a *-automorphism for every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span> if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> is a *-derivation. Conversely a uniformly continuous group of *-automorphisms of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t=e^{td}\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t=e^{td}\\)<\/span> for a unique bounded *-derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>, and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[d=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\alpha_h-I}{h}\\quad\\hbox{in operator norm}.\\]\">\\[d=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\alpha_h-I}{h}\\quad\\hbox{in operator norm}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L34.md","start_offset":15177,"end_offset":15552,"label":"Theorem 5.1","extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"e0205e2511d4d2f0c5df9eb77de12d5756190821ed34d1cad5bd35da05ebed8b":{"markdown":"**Lemma 3.1.** A weakly continuous affine map \\(T:K\\to K\\) on a nonempty weakly compact convex subset of a Banach space has a fixed point.","html":"<p><strong>Lemma 3.1.<\/strong> A weakly continuous affine map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T:K\\to K\\)\">\\(T:K\\to K\\)<\/span> on a nonempty weakly compact convex subset of a Banach space has a fixed point.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FOUND-REMAINDER/src/weakly-compact-convex-sets-and-fixed-points.md","start_offset":9343,"end_offset":9483,"label":"Lemma 3.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0b2282cb3b15c243390fd66b16ba80f083103bde03370c13753e935eae454b4":{"markdown":"**Definition 3.D (subordinate partition).** For an open cover \\((W_i)_{i\\in I}\\) of a topological space \\(X\\), a partition of unity \\((h_j)_{j\\in J}\\) on \\(X\\) is **subordinate** to the cover if for every \\(j\\in J\\) the support of \\(h_j\\) lies in some member \\(W_{i(j)}\\).","html":"<p><strong>Definition 3.D (subordinate partition).<\/strong> For an open cover <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((W_i)_{i\\in I}\\)\">\\((W_i)_{i\\in I}\\)<\/span> of a topological space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, a partition of unity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((h_j)_{j\\in J}\\)\">\\((h_j)_{j\\in J}\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is <strong>subordinate<\/strong> to the cover if for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\in J\\)\">\\(j\\in J\\)<\/span> the support of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_j\\)\">\\(h_j\\)<\/span> lies in some member <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_{i(j)}\\)\">\\(W_{i(j)}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/AG-GS/prerequisites/curated/courses/DG-FND/src/local-tools-for-bundles-and-transport.md","start_offset":41938,"end_offset":42212,"label":"Definition 3.D","extraction_boundary":"current_section_source_reader_compared"},"e07e40668b5d89fde0907eff8390bdd798ce933ce519a54229fabcfd43f07fcb":{"markdown":"**Proposition 8.3** (Exponents). For sets \\(X,Y,Z\\), the map that sends \\(F:Y\\times Z\\to X\\) to \\(z\\mapsto F(\\cdot,z)\\) is a bijection from \\(X^{Y\\times Z}\\) onto \\((X^Y)^Z\\). The map \\((m,n)\\mapsto2^m(2n+1)-1\\) is a bijection \\(\\mathbb N\\times\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\). Consequently \\(|(\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N})^{\\mathbb N}|=|\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N}|\\), that is, \\((2^{\\aleph_0})^{\\aleph_0}=2^{\\aleph_0}\\).","html":"<p><strong>Proposition 8.3<\/strong> (Exponents). For sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X,Y,Z\\)\">\\(X,Y,Z\\)<\/span>, the map that sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F:Y\\times Z\\to X\\)\">\\(F:Y\\times Z\\to X\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\mapsto F(\\cdot,z)\\)\">\\(z\\mapsto F(\\cdot,z)\\)<\/span> is a bijection from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X^{Y\\times Z}\\)\">\\(X^{Y\\times Z}\\)<\/span> onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((X^Y)^Z\\)\">\\((X^Y)^Z\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((m,n)\\mapsto2^m(2n+1)-1\\)\">\\((m,n)\\mapsto2^m(2n+1)-1\\)<\/span> is a bijection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\times\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\times\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|(\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N})^{\\mathbb N}|=|\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N}|\\)\">\\(|(\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N})^{\\mathbb N}|=|\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb N}|\\)<\/span>, that is, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((2^{\\aleph_0})^{\\aleph_0}=2^{\\aleph_0}\\)\">\\((2^{\\aleph_0})^{\\aleph_0}=2^{\\aleph_0}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/hahn-banach-baire-and-the-basic-theorems-on-banach-spaces.md","start_offset":32795,"end_offset":33194,"label":"Proposition 8.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0d9d4741fd572e10eec1f8f0840c5fddfce0fc5bcb48bb4fc4ee610160614ba":{"markdown":"**Exercise 4** (hard; the index group has no torsion). Let \\(A\\) be a commutative nontrivial unital Banach algebra. Show that if \\(x\\in G(A)\\) and \\(x^n\\in G_0(A)\\) for some \\(n\\geq1\\), then \\(x\\in G_0(A)\\).","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 4<\/strong> (hard; the index group has no torsion). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be a commutative nontrivial unital Banach algebra. Show that if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in G(A)\\)\">\\(x\\in G(A)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^n\\in G_0(A)\\)\">\\(x^n\\in G_0(A)\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\geq1\\)\">\\(n\\geq1\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in G_0(A)\\)\">\\(x\\in G_0(A)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/banach-algebras-spectrum-holomorphic-functional-calculus-and-gelfand-theory.md","start_offset":125500,"end_offset":125709,"label":"Exercise 4","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0f8c7a4560b44fc31f3324d96894d66f7eda5d4cf7adb8402a02f848b1054b6":{"markdown":"**Exercise 13.10** (hard) (Ranges of projections of norm one). Let \\(A\\) be a \\(C^*\\)-algebra acting on \\(H\\), let \\(M\\supseteq A\\) be a von Neumann algebra acting on \\(H\\), and let \\(\\varepsilon:M\\to A\\) be a projection of norm one. (a) Prove that each increasing, norm-bounded net \\((h_i)\\) of positive elements of \\(A\\) has a least upper bound in \\(A\\), and that this bound need not be the strong limit. (b) Prove that a separable such \\(A\\) is finite-dimensional.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 13.10<\/strong> (hard) (Ranges of projections of norm one). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> be a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C^*\\)\">\\(C^*\\)<\/span>-algebra acting on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\supseteq A\\)\">\\(M\\supseteq A\\)<\/span> be a von Neumann algebra acting on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon:M\\to A\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon:M\\to A\\)<\/span> be a projection of norm one. (a) Prove that each increasing, norm-bounded net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((h_i)\\)\">\\((h_i)\\)<\/span> of positive elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has a least upper bound in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, and that this bound need not be the strong limit. (b) Prove that a separable such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is finite-dimensional.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/the-universal-enveloping-von-neumann-algebra-of-a-c-star-algebra-and-w-star-algebras.md","start_offset":154564,"end_offset":155033,"label":"Exercise 13.10","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0c8ab79fe9dc65bf81cc3f5454969e90cc2aefad3f96be65de19ef2f4ad17fd":{"markdown":"**Proposition 5.2.**\n1. Borel sets are both Souslin and co-Souslin. A set that is both Souslin and co-Souslin is Borel.\n2. Countable unions and countable intersections of co-Souslin sets are co-Souslin.\n3. If \\(f:X\\to Y\\) is Borel and \\(C\\subseteq Y\\) is co-Souslin, then \\(f^{-1}(C)\\) is co-Souslin.\n4. If \\(S\\subseteq X\\times Y\\) is Souslin, its projection \\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in S\\text{ for some }y\\}\\) is Souslin. If \\(C\\subseteq X\\times Y\\) is co-Souslin, then \\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in C\\text{ for all }y\\}\\) is co-Souslin.\n5. Some co-Souslin set in \\(\\mathcal C\\) is not a Souslin set, namely \\(\\mathcal C\\setminus D\\) for the set \\(D\\) of (D4).","html":"<p><strong>Proposition 5.2.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Borel sets are both Souslin and co-Souslin. A set that is both Souslin and co-Souslin is Borel.<\/li>\n<li>Countable unions and countable intersections of co-Souslin sets are co-Souslin.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:X\\to Y\\)\">\\(f:X\\to Y\\)<\/span> is Borel and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\subseteq Y\\)\">\\(C\\subseteq Y\\)<\/span> is co-Souslin, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f^{-1}(C)\\)\">\\(f^{-1}(C)\\)<\/span> is co-Souslin.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\subseteq X\\times Y\\)\">\\(S\\subseteq X\\times Y\\)<\/span> is Souslin, its projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in S\\text{ for some }y\\}\\)\">\\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in S\\text{ for some }y\\}\\)<\/span> is Souslin. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\subseteq X\\times Y\\)\">\\(C\\subseteq X\\times Y\\)<\/span> is co-Souslin, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in C\\text{ for all }y\\}\\)\">\\(\\{x:(x,y)\\in C\\text{ for all }y\\}\\)<\/span> is co-Souslin.<\/li>\n<li>Some co-Souslin set in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C\\)<\/span> is not a Souslin set, namely <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal C\\setminus D\\)\">\\(\\mathcal C\\setminus D\\)<\/span> for the set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> of (D4).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/numerical-ranges-positive-matrices-and-co-souslin-sets.md","start_offset":61739,"end_offset":62374,"label":"Proposition 5.2","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0d2ad9c42dac43e8941faf2f3c40ee6da18aca66f08c39f6f6d5e995a298875":{"markdown":"**Exercise 12.3** (medium; Minimal projections and isolated points). Let \\(M\\) be a commutative von Neumann algebra, with spectrum \\(\\Omega\\). For a projection \\(e\\in M\\) let \\(C_e\\) be the clopen set on which its transform is \\(1\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(e\\ne0\\) is a minimal projection if and only if \\(C_e\\) is a single point, which is then isolated.\n\n(b) Show that \\(M\\) is *atomic*, that is, every nonzero projection majorizes a minimal projection, if and only if \\(\\Omega=\\Omega_d\\) in Proposition 6.5, and that then \\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\), where \\(I\\) is the set of minimal projections.\n\n(c) Show that \\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\oplus M_c\\), where the abelian von Neumann algebra \\(M_c\\) has no minimal projections; \\(I\\) may be empty and \\(M_c\\) may be \\(0\\).\n\n(d) Show that the spectrum of \\(L^\\infty[0,1]\\) has no isolated points.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 12.3<\/strong> (medium; Minimal projections and isolated points). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> be a commutative von Neumann algebra, with spectrum <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span>. For a projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\in M\\)\">\\(e\\in M\\)<\/span> let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_e\\)\">\\(C_e\\)<\/span> be the clopen set on which its transform is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(a) Show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\ne0\\)\">\\(e\\ne0\\)<\/span> is a minimal projection if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_e\\)\">\\(C_e\\)<\/span> is a single point, which is then isolated.<\/p>\n<p>(b) Show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is <em>atomic<\/em>, that is, every nonzero projection majorizes a minimal projection, if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega=\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega=\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> in Proposition 6.5, and that then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)\">\\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is the set of minimal projections.<\/p>\n<p>(c) Show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\oplus M_c\\)\">\\(M\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\oplus M_c\\)<\/span>, where the abelian von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_c\\)\">\\(M_c\\)<\/span> has no minimal projections; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> may be empty and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_c\\)\">\\(M_c\\)<\/span> may be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(d) Show that the spectrum of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty[0,1]\\)\">\\(L^\\infty[0,1]\\)<\/span> has no isolated points.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/abelian-operator-algebras.md","start_offset":153429,"end_offset":154264,"label":"Exercise 12.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0442cc113b605f4d9fd3529299a842591f2218ab4beca88b229edfd88c686a7":{"markdown":"**Exercise 14.4.** (hard) Projections \\(e,f\\) are *unitarily equivalent* if \\(ueu^*=f\\) for a unitary \\(u\\in M\\); then \\(e\\sim f\\). Show that if \\(e\\sim f\\), there are orthogonal decompositions \\(e=\\sum_ie_i\\) and \\(f=\\sum_if_i\\) such that \\(e_i\\) and \\(f_i\\) are unitarily equivalent for each \\(i\\).","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 14.4.<\/strong> (hard) Projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e,f\\)\">\\(e,f\\)<\/span> are <em>unitarily equivalent<\/em> if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ueu^*=f\\)\">\\(ueu^*=f\\)<\/span> for a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in M\\)\">\\(u\\in M\\)<\/span>; then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\sim f\\)\">\\(e\\sim f\\)<\/span>. Show that if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\sim f\\)\">\\(e\\sim f\\)<\/span>, there are orthogonal decompositions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e=\\sum_ie_i\\)\">\\(e=\\sum_ie_i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=\\sum_if_i\\)\">\\(f=\\sum_if_i\\)<\/span> such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_i\\)\">\\(e_i\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_i\\)\">\\(f_i\\)<\/span> are unitarily equivalent for each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.md","start_offset":98228,"end_offset":98530,"label":"Exercise 14.4","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e01bff0f52e8c7bc229f509e42b6b4ca22c7d4d641f8d1a881eec3f966b35e40":{"markdown":"**Example 3.3** (Where the partial isometry lives). Let \\(A=C[0,1]\\) and \\(\\lambda(x)=\\int_0^1x\\,dt\\).\n\n1. For \\(s\\ne t\\) in \\([0,1]\\), \\(|\\delta_s-\\delta_t|=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\). Indeed, \\(\\omega=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\) is positive with \\(\\|\\omega\\|=2=\\|\\delta_s-\\delta_t\\|\\) (test on a continuous \\(x\\) with \\(|x|\\le1\\), \\(x(s)=1\\), \\(x(t)=-1\\)), and \\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|x(s)-x(t)|^2\\\\\n\\le2(|x(s)|^2+|x(t)|^2)\\\\\n=2\\,\\omega(xx^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\] Theorem 3.1 applies.\n2. Let \\(\\sigma=1_{[0,1/2)}-1_{[1/2,1]}\\) and \\(f(x)=\\int_0^1x\\sigma\\,dt\\). Then \\(\\|f\\|=1\\): the bound \\(\\le\\) is clear, and continuous functions \\(x_n\\) with \\(|x_n|\\le1\\) that equal \\(\\sigma\\) outside an interval of length \\(1/n\\) give \\(f(x_n)\\ge1-2/n\\). Also \\(\\|\\lambda\\|=1\\) and \\(|f(x)|^2\\le\\big(\\int|x|\\big)^2\\le\\int|x|^2=\\lambda(xx^*)\\). By Theorem 3.1, \\(|f|=\\lambda\\). Now suppose the partial isometry \\(v\\) of \\(f\\) lay in \\(A\\), say \\(v=a\\). Then \\(f(x)=\\lambda(xa)=\\int_0^1xa\\,dt\\) for all \\(x\\in A\\). Take \\(x\\ge0\\) continuous, supported in \\([\\tfrac12-\\delta,\\tfrac12]\\), with \\(\\int x=1\\). Then \\(1=f(x)=\\int xa\\), and as \\(\\delta\\to0\\) this tends to \\(a(\\tfrac12)\\), by continuity of \\(a\\). So \\(a(\\tfrac12)=1\\). The same test with support in \\([\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12+\\delta]\\) gives \\(a(\\tfrac12)=-1\\), a contradiction. So \\(v\\notin A\\).\n3. Even for positive functionals, \\(v=s(f)\\) need not lie in \\(A\\). Let \\(A=K(H)\\), whose bidual is \\(B(H)\\) (background fact 14), let \\((e_n)\\) be an infinite orthonormal sequence, and let \\(f=\\sum_n2^{-n}\\omega_{e_n}\\). Its normal extension to \\(B(H)\\) is given by the same formula. Its support is the projection \\(P\\) onto the closed span of the \\(e_n\\): \\(f(1-P)=0\\), and a projection \\(q\\le P\\) with \\(f(q)=0\\) has \\(qe_n=0\\) for all \\(n\\), so \\(q=0\\). So \\(v=P\\) has infinite rank and is not compact.\n\nThe next result replaces the triangle inequality, which fails for absolute values (Example 3.5).","html":"<p><strong>Example 3.3<\/strong> (Where the partial isometry lives). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=C[0,1]\\)\">\\(A=C[0,1]\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda(x)=\\int_0^1x\\,dt\\)\">\\(\\lambda(x)=\\int_0^1x\\,dt\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\ne t\\)\">\\(s\\ne t\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1]\\)\">\\([0,1]\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\delta_s-\\delta_t|=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\)\">\\(|\\delta_s-\\delta_t|=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\)<\/span>. Indeed, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\)\">\\(\\omega=\\delta_s+\\delta_t\\)<\/span> is positive with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\omega\\|=2=\\|\\delta_s-\\delta_t\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\omega\\|=2=\\|\\delta_s-\\delta_t\\|\\)<\/span> (test on a continuous <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x|\\le1\\)\">\\(|x|\\le1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(s)=1\\)\">\\(x(s)=1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x(t)=-1\\)\">\\(x(t)=-1\\)<\/span>), and <span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|x(s)-x(t)|^2\\\\\n\\le2(|x(s)|^2+|x(t)|^2)\\\\\n=2\\,\\omega(xx^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n|x(s)-x(t)|^2\\\\\n\\le2(|x(s)|^2+|x(t)|^2)\\\\\n=2\\,\\omega(xx^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span> Theorem 3.1 applies.<\/li>\n<li>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma=1_{[0,1/2)}-1_{[1/2,1]}\\)\">\\(\\sigma=1_{[0,1/2)}-1_{[1/2,1]}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)=\\int_0^1x\\sigma\\,dt\\)\">\\(f(x)=\\int_0^1x\\sigma\\,dt\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|f\\|=1\\)\">\\(\\|f\\|=1\\)<\/span>: the bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\le\\)\">\\(\\le\\)<\/span> is clear, and continuous functions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n\\)\">\\(x_n\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x_n|\\le1\\)\">\\(|x_n|\\le1\\)<\/span> that equal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> outside an interval of length <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/n\\)\">\\(1/n\\)<\/span> give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x_n)\\ge1-2/n\\)\">\\(f(x_n)\\ge1-2/n\\)<\/span>. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\lambda\\|=1\\)\">\\(\\|\\lambda\\|=1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|f(x)|^2\\le\\big(\\int|x|\\big)^2\\le\\int|x|^2=\\lambda(xx^*)\\)\">\\(|f(x)|^2\\le\\big(\\int|x|\\big)^2\\le\\int|x|^2=\\lambda(xx^*)\\)<\/span>. By Theorem 3.1, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|f|=\\lambda\\)\">\\(|f|=\\lambda\\)<\/span>. Now suppose the partial isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> lay in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, say <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=a\\)\">\\(v=a\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)=\\lambda(xa)=\\int_0^1xa\\,dt\\)\">\\(f(x)=\\lambda(xa)=\\int_0^1xa\\,dt\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in A\\)\">\\(x\\in A\\)<\/span>. Take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\ge0\\)\">\\(x\\ge0\\)<\/span> continuous, supported in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\tfrac12-\\delta,\\tfrac12]\\)\">\\([\\tfrac12-\\delta,\\tfrac12]\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int x=1\\)\">\\(\\int x=1\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1=f(x)=\\int xa\\)\">\\(1=f(x)=\\int xa\\)<\/span>, and as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta\\to0\\)\">\\(\\delta\\to0\\)<\/span> this tends to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(\\tfrac12)\\)\">\\(a(\\tfrac12)\\)<\/span>, by continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(\\tfrac12)=1\\)\">\\(a(\\tfrac12)=1\\)<\/span>. The same test with support in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12+\\delta]\\)\">\\([\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12+\\delta]\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a(\\tfrac12)=-1\\)\">\\(a(\\tfrac12)=-1\\)<\/span>, a contradiction. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\notin A\\)\">\\(v\\notin A\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Even for positive functionals, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=s(f)\\)\">\\(v=s(f)\\)<\/span> need not lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=K(H)\\)\">\\(A=K(H)\\)<\/span>, whose bidual is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span> (background fact 14), let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e_n)\\)\">\\((e_n)\\)<\/span> be an infinite orthonormal sequence, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=\\sum_n2^{-n}\\omega_{e_n}\\)\">\\(f=\\sum_n2^{-n}\\omega_{e_n}\\)<\/span>. Its normal extension to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span> is given by the same formula. Its support is the projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> onto the closed span of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_n\\)\">\\(e_n\\)<\/span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(1-P)=0\\)\">\\(f(1-P)=0\\)<\/span>, and a projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\le P\\)\">\\(q\\le P\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(q)=0\\)\">\\(f(q)=0\\)<\/span> has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(qe_n=0\\)\">\\(qe_n=0\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=0\\)\">\\(q=0\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=P\\)\">\\(v=P\\)<\/span> has infinite rank and is not compact.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The next result replaces the triangle inequality, which fails for absolute values (Example 3.5).<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/polar-decomposition-of-functionals-and-weak-compactness-in-preduals.md","start_offset":53233,"end_offset":55160,"label":"Example 3.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"e0988a9d2e01c30dcf3b02ff4369d8a6e89c298aa7686a444259c471c348fefa":{"markdown":"**Lemma.** Let \\(E\\) be a real Banach space, \\(K\\subseteq E\\) a nonempty weakly compact convex set, and \\(G\\) a group of surjective linear isometries of \\(E\\) with \\(gK=K\\). Then some point of \\(K\\) is fixed by every \\(g\\in G\\).\n\n**6.1. One bounded linear map.** If a bounded linear map \\(F:E\\to E\\) satisfies \\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\), fix \\(x_0\\in K\\) and put\n\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]\nThe set \\(K\\) is norm bounded: weak compactness gives pointwise boundedness on \\(E^*\\), and the Uniform Boundedness Theorem applies to the canonical images in \\(E^{**}\\). Write \\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\). Then\n\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]\nA weakly convergent subnet has limit \\(x\\in K\\). Bounded linear maps are weakly continuous, so \\(Fx=x\\).\n\n**6.2. Small caps in a separable space.** Suppose that \\(E\\) is separable and \\(K\\) has more than one point. For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a proper weakly compact convex subset \\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nHere and below closures in this substep are weak closures.\n\nLet \\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\). By [WT, Theorem 6.1](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06), \\(L\\) is nonempty and compact and \\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\). Separability provides a countable cover of \\(E\\) by closed norm balls of radius \\(\\varepsilon/3\\). Such balls are weakly closed, by Hahn–Banach separation. The Baire theorem for compact Hausdorff spaces, applied to \\(L\\), gives a ball \\(B_0\\) for which \\(L\\cap B_0\\) contains a nonempty relatively weakly open subset \\(U\\) of \\(L\\).\n\nIf \\(L\\setminus U\\) is empty, then \\(K\\subseteq B_0\\), so \\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\). Any singleton in \\(K\\) is a proper subset satisfying (6.1).\n\nOtherwise put\n\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]\nBoth are nonempty compact convex subsets of \\(K\\), and \\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\). The convex join\n\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]\nis compact, convex, and contains \\(L\\); it is therefore \\(K\\).\n\nChoose \\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\), which exists because \\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\) is dense in \\(L\\). We have \\(y\\notin K_1\\). Indeed, otherwise \\(y\\) would be extreme in \\(K_1\\), and the converse to Krein–Milman [WT, Theorem 6.2](../../weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06), applied to the compact set \\(L\\setminus U\\), would give \\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\).\n\nFor \\(0<\\eta<1\\), define\n\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]\nThe equality follows by substituting the preceding description of \\(K\\) into the first expression. This set is compact and convex. It does not contain \\(y\\): an expression \\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\), with \\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\), would force \\(v=y\\) by extremality.\n\nIf \\(x,x'\\notin K_\\eta\\), write\n\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x'=t'v'+(1-t')w'\n\\]\nwith \\(v,v'\\in K_1\\), \\(w,w'\\in K_2\\). Necessarily \\(t,t'<\\eta\\). Since \\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|>0\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x'\\|\n&\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w'\\|+t'\\|v'-w'\\|\\\\\n&\\le2R(t+t')+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\eta<\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\). Then \\(K_0=K_\\eta\\) proves (6.1).\n\n**6.3. Finitely many isometries.** Given \\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\), apply 6.1 to \\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\), obtaining \\(x\\in K\\) with\n\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]\nIf some, but not all, of the \\(g_j\\) fix \\(x\\), cancel their contributions and renumber. To obtain a contradiction, we may thus assume\n\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThe subgroup \\(G_0\\) generated by these finitely many isometries is countable. Replace \\(K\\) by the weakly closed convex hull of \\(G_0x\\). This hull is a weakly compact subset of the original \\(K\\). It lies in the norm-closed linear span \\(E_0\\) of the countable orbit: a norm-closed linear subspace is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach. The space \\(E_0\\) is separable, and its weak topology is the subspace weak topology, again by Hahn–Banach extension. Thus 6.2 applies.\n\nChoose\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]\nand a set \\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\) satisfying (6.1). Some \\(hx\\), \\(h\\in G_0\\), lies outside \\(K_0\\), since otherwise the closed convex hull of the orbit would be contained in \\(K_0\\). Equation (6.2) gives\n\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]\nConvexity of \\(K_0\\) implies that \\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\) for some \\(j\\). But then\n\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]\ncontrary to the choice of \\(\\varepsilon\\). Therefore the original \\(x\\) is fixed by all \\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\).\n\n**6.4. An arbitrary group.** For each \\(g\\), its fixed-point set in \\(K\\) is weakly closed. Substep 6.3 proves the finite intersection property for these sets. Compactness gives a point in their intersection. This proves the lemma.\n\nThe fixed-point argument follows [L26, Theorem 1, Proposition 2 and Lemma 3].","html":"<p><strong>Lemma.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a real Banach space, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subseteq E\\)\">\\(K\\subseteq E\\)<\/span> a nonempty weakly compact convex set, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> a group of surjective linear isometries of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gK=K\\)\">\\(gK=K\\)<\/span>. Then some point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is fixed by every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\in G\\)\">\\(g\\in G\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.1. One bounded linear map.<\/strong> If a bounded linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F:E\\to E\\)\">\\(F:E\\to E\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\)\">\\(F(K)\\subseteq K\\)<\/span>, fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_0\\in K\\)\">\\(x_0\\in K\\)<\/span> and put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]\">\\[\nx_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^jx_0.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is norm bounded: weak compactness gives pointwise boundedness on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E^*\\)\">\\(E^*\\)<\/span>, and the Uniform Boundedness Theorem applies to the canonical images in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E^{**}\\)\">\\(E^{**}\\)<\/span>. Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(R=\\sup_{x\\in K}\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]\">\\[\nx_n\\in K,\\qquad\n\\|Fx_n-x_n\\|=\\frac1n\\|F^nx_0-x_0\\|\\le\\frac{2R}{n}.\n\\]<\/span>\nA weakly convergent subnet has limit <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K\\)\">\\(x\\in K\\)<\/span>. Bounded linear maps are weakly continuous, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Fx=x\\)\">\\(Fx=x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.2. Small caps in a separable space.<\/strong> Suppose that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> is separable and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> has more than one point. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>, there is a proper weakly compact convex subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)\">\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)<\/span> such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(K\\setminus K_0)\\le\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere and below closures in this substep are weak closures.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\)\">\\(L=\\overline{\\operatorname{Ext}K}\\)<\/span>. By <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06\">WT, Theorem 6.1<\/a>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> is nonempty and compact and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\)\">\\(K=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}L\\)<\/span>. Separability provides a countable cover of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> by closed norm balls of radius <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon/3\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon/3\\)<\/span>. Such balls are weakly closed, by Hahn–Banach separation. The Baire theorem for compact Hausdorff spaces, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>, gives a ball <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B_0\\)\">\\(B_0\\)<\/span> for which <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\cap B_0\\)\">\\(L\\cap B_0\\)<\/span> contains a nonempty relatively weakly open subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(L\\setminus U\\)<\/span> is empty, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\subseteq B_0\\)\">\\(K\\subseteq B_0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{diam}K\\le2\\varepsilon/3\\)<\/span>. Any singleton in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is a proper subset satisfying (6.1).<\/p>\n<p>Otherwise put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]\">\\[\nK_1=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\setminus U),\\qquad\nK_2=\\overline{\\operatorname{co}}(L\\cap B_0).\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth are nonempty compact convex subsets of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\)\">\\(K_2\\subseteq B_0\\)<\/span>. The convex join\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1\\}\n\\]<\/span>\nis compact, convex, and contains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>; it is therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\)\">\\(y\\in\\operatorname{Ext}K\\cap U\\)<\/span>, which exists because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ext}K\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span>. We have <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\notin K_1\\)\">\\(y\\notin K_1\\)<\/span>. Indeed, otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span> would be extreme in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_1\\)\">\\(K_1\\)<\/span>, and the converse to Krein–Milman <a href=\"../courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/weak-topologies-tychonoff-banach-alaoglu-mazur-bipolars-krein-milman-and-eberlein-smulian.html#OA-FND-WT-06\">WT, Theorem 6.2<\/a>, applied to the compact set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(L\\setminus U\\)<\/span>, would give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\)\">\\(y\\in L\\setminus U\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]\">\\[\nK_\\eta=\\eta K_1+(1-\\eta)K\n=\\{tv+(1-t)w:v\\in K_1,\\ w\\in K_2,\\ \\eta\\le t\\le1\\}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe equality follows by substituting the preceding description of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> into the first expression. This set is compact and convex. It does not contain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span>: an expression <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\)\">\\(y=\\eta v+(1-\\eta)k\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\)\">\\(v\\in K_1,k\\in K\\)<\/span>, would force <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=y\\)\">\\(v=y\\)<\/span> by extremality.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,x&#x27;\\notin K_\\eta\\)\">\\(x,x&#x27;\\notin K_\\eta\\)<\/span>, write\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x&#x27;=t&#x27;v&#x27;+(1-t&#x27;)w&#x27;\n\\]\">\\[\nx=tv+(1-t)w,\\qquad x&#x27;=t&#x27;v&#x27;+(1-t&#x27;)w&#x27;\n\\]<\/span>\nwith <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v,v&#x27;\\in K_1\\)\">\\(v,v&#x27;\\in K_1\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w,w&#x27;\\in K_2\\)\">\\(w,w&#x27;\\in K_2\\)<\/span>. Necessarily <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t,t&#x27;&lt;\\eta\\)\">\\(t,t&#x27;&lt;\\eta\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|&gt;0\\)\">\\(R=\\sup_K\\|x\\|&gt;0\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x&#x27;\\|\n&amp;\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w&#x27;\\|+t&#x27;\\|v&#x27;-w&#x27;\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le2R(t+t&#x27;)+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|x-x&#x27;\\|\n&amp;\\le t\\|v-w\\|+\\|w-w&#x27;\\|+t&#x27;\\|v&#x27;-w&#x27;\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le2R(t+t&#x27;)+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}\n\\le4R\\eta+\\frac{2\\varepsilon}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\nChoose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta&lt;\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\)\">\\(\\eta&lt;\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon/(12R)\\}\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0=K_\\eta\\)\">\\(K_0=K_\\eta\\)<\/span> proves (6.1).<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.3. Finitely many isometries.<\/strong> Given <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\)\">\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\in G\\)<\/span>, apply 6.1 to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\)\">\\(F=n^{-1}\\sum_jg_j\\)<\/span>, obtaining <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in K\\)\">\\(x\\in K\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]\">\\[\nx=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ng_jx.\n\\]<\/span>\nIf some, but not all, of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_j\\)\">\\(g_j\\)<\/span> fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, cancel their contributions and renumber. To obtain a contradiction, we may thus assume\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\">\\[\nx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^mg_jx,\n\\qquad g_jx\\ne x\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe subgroup <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_0\\)\">\\(G_0\\)<\/span> generated by these finitely many isometries is countable. Replace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> by the weakly closed convex hull of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_0x\\)\">\\(G_0x\\)<\/span>. This hull is a weakly compact subset of the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>. It lies in the norm-closed linear span <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\)<\/span> of the countable orbit: a norm-closed linear subspace is weakly closed by Hahn–Banach. The space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_0\\)\">\\(E_0\\)<\/span> is separable, and its weak topology is the subspace weak topology, again by Hahn–Banach extension. Thus 6.2 applies.<\/p>\n<p>Choose\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]\">\\[\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}\\|g_jx-x\\|\n\\]<\/span>\nand a set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)\">\\(K_0\\subsetneq K\\)<\/span> satisfying (6.1). Some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hx\\)\">\\(hx\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\in G_0\\)\">\\(h\\in G_0\\)<\/span>, lies outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span>, since otherwise the closed convex hull of the orbit would be contained in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span>. Equation (6.2) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]\">\\[\nhx=\\frac1m\\sum_{j=1}^m hg_jx.\n\\]<\/span>\nConvexity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_0\\)\">\\(K_0\\)<\/span> implies that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\)\">\\(hg_jx\\notin K_0\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span>. But then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]\">\\[\n\\|g_jx-x\\|=\\|hg_jx-hx\\|\\le\\varepsilon,\n\\]<\/span>\ncontrary to the choice of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon\\)<\/span>. Therefore the original <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> is fixed by all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\)\">\\(g_1,\\dots,g_n\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>6.4. An arbitrary group.<\/strong> For each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, its fixed-point set in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is weakly closed. Substep 6.3 proves the finite intersection property for these sets. Compactness gives a point in their intersection. This proves the lemma.<\/p>\n<p>The fixed-point argument follows [L26, Theorem 1, Proposition 2 and Lemma 3].<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/support/src/derivations-of-von-neumann-algebras-alternative.md","start_offset":19440,"end_offset":24519,"label":null,"extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"e06cdb6dac18a0d855d6039653f13ed96cb6a5be4c57b123b854d3d76c610388":{"markdown":"**Examples 1.6.**\n1. For \\(H\\ne\\{0\\}\\), the identity representation of \\(B(H)\\) on \\(H\\) is nondegenerate, and every nonzero vector \\(\\xi\\) is cyclic, since \\(\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\xi=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\eta\\) for every \\(\\eta\\).\n2. Let \\(X\\) be a locally compact Hausdorff space, and let \\(C_0(X)\\) act on \\(\\ell^2(X)\\) by \\((\\pi(f)\\xi)(x)=f(x)\\xi(x)\\). Each line \\(\\mathbb C\\delta_x\\) is invariant, and \\(\\pi\\) is the direct sum of the one-dimensional representations \\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\). It is nondegenerate, because by Urysohn's lemma each \\(x\\) has some \\(f\\) with \\(f(x)\\ne0\\), and it is faithful, because \\(\\pi(f)\\delta_x=f(x)\\delta_x\\). If \\(X\\) is uncountable, \\(\\pi\\) is not cyclic: a vector \\(\\xi\\in\\ell^2(X)\\) is supported on a countable set, and so is every \\(\\pi(f)\\xi\\).\n3. The representation \\(x\\mapsto x\\oplus0\\) of \\(M_n(\\mathbb C)\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^n\\oplus\\mathbb C\\) is degenerate, with null space \\(0\\oplus\\mathbb C\\). Here the image of the identity is the projection onto the essential subspace, as in Proposition 1.4(4).","html":"<p><strong>Examples 1.6.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\ne\\{0\\}\\)\">\\(H\\ne\\{0\\}\\)<\/span>, the identity representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is nondegenerate, and every nonzero vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\)\">\\(\\xi\\)<\/span> is cyclic, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\xi=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\eta\\)\">\\(\\theta_{\\eta,\\xi}\\xi=\\|\\xi\\|^2\\eta\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> be a locally compact Hausdorff space, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_0(X)\\)\">\\(C_0(X)\\)<\/span> act on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(X)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(X)\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\pi(f)\\xi)(x)=f(x)\\xi(x)\\)\">\\((\\pi(f)\\xi)(x)=f(x)\\xi(x)\\)<\/span>. Each line <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\delta_x\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\delta_x\\)<\/span> is invariant, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is the direct sum of the one-dimensional representations <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\)\">\\(f\\mapsto f(x)\\)<\/span>. It is nondegenerate, because by Urysohn's lemma each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> has some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(x)\\ne0\\)\">\\(f(x)\\ne0\\)<\/span>, and it is faithful, because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(f)\\delta_x=f(x)\\delta_x\\)\">\\(\\pi(f)\\delta_x=f(x)\\delta_x\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> is uncountable, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span> is not cyclic: a vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi\\in\\ell^2(X)\\)\">\\(\\xi\\in\\ell^2(X)\\)<\/span> is supported on a countable set, and so is every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(f)\\xi\\)\">\\(\\pi(f)\\xi\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>The representation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\mapsto x\\oplus0\\)\">\\(x\\mapsto x\\oplus0\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_n(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M_n(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^n\\oplus\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^n\\oplus\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> is degenerate, with null space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\oplus\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(0\\oplus\\mathbb C\\)<\/span>. Here the image of the identity is the projection onto the essential subspace, as in Proposition 1.4(4).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/representations-and-positive-functionals-the-gns-construction-and-the-gelfand-naimark.md","start_offset":13115,"end_offset":14141,"label":null,"extraction_boundary":"registered_full_statement_exact_source_match"}});
